WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.320
Nobody is safe in the world of The&nbsp;
Walking Dead — and even whole scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.200
can be devoured alive. Here's the&nbsp;
ending you'll never get to see.

00:00:07.672 --> 00:00:12.240
After 11 seasons, The Walking Dead is finally&nbsp;
resting in peace. The ending the program gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.680
loyal viewers was nothing if not bittersweet:&nbsp;
While the majority of its regulars survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.720
Rosita Espinosa, Luke, and Jules all succumb&nbsp;
to walker bites. But the rest of the cast of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.580
characters thrive and find family, happiness,&nbsp;
and romance. Their settlements grow in peace as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.260
well. The show's three upcoming spin-offs are&nbsp;
set up, too, with Daryl Dixon choosing to roam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.760
the country protecting others, Negan taking off&nbsp;
for New York City, and Rick Grimes and Michonne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.320
both seen fighting for their lives miles apart&nbsp;
in completely different locations. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.940
Judith Grimes and her half-brother, RJ, continue&nbsp;
to wait at Hilltop for their parents' arrival.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.400
"You deserve a happy ending, too."

00:00:51.674 --> 00:00:52.536
"I'll be back."

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.460
While this was an ending that&nbsp;
moved many in the show's audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.720
it turns out that the series had planned an&nbsp;
entirely different conclusion to the adventures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.220
of our intrepid survivors. This ending inspires&nbsp;
hope for the future in a completely different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.120
way from the aired version and cements the&nbsp;
survival of several Walking Dead characters.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.920
Per an article published by Insider, The Walking&nbsp;
Dead was initially slated to end with a peek into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.540
the future. Greg Nicotero, an executive producer&nbsp;
and director of the series, revealed during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.340
Zoom call that the series finale we saw was not&nbsp;
necessarily the planned series finale. He said:

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.560
Insider proceeded to confirm this deleted&nbsp;
scene through two members of the show's crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.740
who preferred to remain anonymous.&nbsp;
According to them, the final scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.040
would have taken us back to Atlanta,&nbsp;
Georgia — specifically, Freedom Parkway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.360
down which Rick Grimes rode on horseback&nbsp;
during the show's first episode. But this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.320
the characters traveling down the highway are&nbsp;
in a van. The scene would be shot in such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.060
manner as to reveal that these characters are&nbsp;
now-grown Judith, RJ, and their friends. They are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.200
just like their parents, apparently on a mission&nbsp;
to rescue others from the apocalyptic battlefront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.000
and reunify disenfranchised communities. It's&nbsp;
hinted that some progress has continued to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.140
made technologically as well, as the van&nbsp;
apparently runs on ethanol. This grown-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.940
version of RJ speaks into a longwave radio,&nbsp;
calling out for any survivors. The episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.840
and the show, was to end with an unseen&nbsp;
survivor speaking back through the radio.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.400
So why didn't AMC end The Walking Dead&nbsp;
somewhere in the far-flung future? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.780
crew members Insider spoke to gave reasons&nbsp;
that mesh with Nicotero's — it simply didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.380
serve the franchise's future health.&nbsp;
While one crew member said that ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.500
the show with new versions of characters&nbsp;
we weren't invested in didn't make sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.760
another mentioned that the original ending&nbsp;
was simply more fitting, emotionally-speaking

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.900
This all stems from the fact that the comic&nbsp;
book version of The Walking Dead also ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.580
with a 25-year time jump. The focus of that&nbsp;
final issue is Carl Grimes, who, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.000
did not survive until the show's end in&nbsp;
the television universe. While neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.660
version places a neat bow on the show and&nbsp;
leaves things open for further sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.920
it's understandable why AMC felt the need&nbsp;
to establish Rick and Michonne's upcoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.220
sequel. Still, it's hard not to be&nbsp;
curious about what could have been.

